**Symmetrical**: the left and right halves of the whole display are close to mirror images. A peaked symmetrical distribution has equal tails on both sides, so neither side has a longer tail.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,4.1);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.6,0);
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1.5,0.4);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2.5,1.2);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3.5,2.4);
\draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.5,3.2);
\draw (4.5,0) rectangle (5.5,2.4);
\draw (5.5,0) rectangle (6.5,1.2);
\draw (6.5,0) rectangle (7.5,0.4);
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.7,2.0) {Frequency};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,-0.2) {Value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Negatively skewed**: the tail stretches to the **left**, so most of the data sits on the **right** (some books call this left-skewed).

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,4.9);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.6,0);
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1.5,0.4);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2.5,0.8);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3.5,1.2);
\draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.5,2);
\draw (4.5,0) rectangle (5.5,3.2);
\draw (5.5,0) rectangle (6.5,4);
\draw (6.5,0) rectangle (7.5,2.8);
\draw[->,thick] (3.3,2.4)--(0.7,2.4);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (2,2.4) {tail};
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.7,2.5) {Frequency};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,-0.2) {Value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Positively skewed**: the tail stretches to the **right**, so most of the data sits on the **left** (some books call this right-skewed).

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,4.9);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.6,0);
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1.5,2.8);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2.5,4);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3.5,3.2);
\draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.5,2);
\draw (4.5,0) rectangle (5.5,1.2);
\draw (5.5,0) rectangle (6.5,0.8);
\draw (6.5,0) rectangle (7.5,0.4);
\draw[->,thick] (4.7,2.4)--(7.3,2.4);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (6,2.4) {tail};
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.7,2.5) {Frequency};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,-0.2) {Value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Uniform**: the frequencies are approximately equal, so the display is flat with no distinct peak. An exactly flat display is symmetrical because its halves are mirror images, and it has no tapering tail on either side.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.9);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.6,0);
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1.5,2);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2.5,2);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3.5,2);
\draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.5,2);
\draw (4.5,0) rectangle (5.5,2);
\draw (5.5,0) rectangle (6.5,2);
\draw (6.5,0) rectangle (7.5,2);
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.7,1.4) {Frequency};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,-0.2) {Value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Unimodal** means one distinct peak. **Bimodal** means two separated peaks, and **multimodal** means several peaks. Separated peaks do not need to have equal heights. Two matching peaks alone do not establish symmetry: the whole display must have approximately mirrored halves. This display is both bimodal and symmetrical.

